  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nA few showers are ongoing this afternoon across far southern\nMinnesota on the northern edge of a theta-e gradient along the\nIowa border. Surface high pressure centered over the Great Lakes\nthis afternoon will shift east tonight and allow deeper moisture\ntransport to begin overnight. Can't rule out a few showers\nlingering across southern MN this evening before expanding in\ncoverage and northern extent overnight into Saturday morning.\nWith the lack of any decent and focused forcing, these warm air\nadvection showers should remain scattered and brief - nothing \nthat will washout Halloween activities. Otherwise, the last day\nof October will feature light southeast winds, cloudy skies, and\ntemperatures in the 50s. Can't completely rule out a few random\nlight showers around for the rest of the weekend with a weak \nshortwave lingering in the vicinity through Sunday, but by this\npoint the moisture transport will have waned to a degree the\nodds of staying dry far outweigh the off chance of seeing a \nbrief period of rain. \n\nModel consistency has improved significantly from a couple of\ndays ago for early next week. A large and deep trough pushing \nonshore the West Coast Saturday will reach the Rockies Sunday \nand the Plains Monday. With surface high pressure parked over \nthe mid Atlantic states through the western Atlantic Ocean, a \ndeep and persistent fetch of tropical moisture will advect north\nin advance of the approaching trough. Also, from the Pacific, a\ntropical wave will be picked up by this trough while it reaches\nthe West Coast. Pwats exceeding 1.5 inches will begin reaching \nthe central Plains by Sunday evening, and then will reach the \nUpper Midwest Monday morning. These values are 4-4.5 standard \ndeviations above normal. A 50+ kt 850 mb jet with this degree of\nmoisture will lead to 300+ gm/kgs moisture transport magnitudes\nthroughout the day. A surface low will track northeast across \nthe central Plains to somewhere across eastern SD or western MN,\npossibly stall and meandering for a time, then continue to push\noff to the northeast as it interacts with a secondary \ndisturbance. Moderate to heavy rain will arc from the northern \nto eastern quadrants of the system, ensuring the entire forecast\narea is likely to see beneficial rains. How fast this \nprecipitation moves through and the track of the meandering low \nwill ultimately dictate rainfall totals, but chances are solid \nfor widespread 1 to 2 inches with about a 10 percent chance of \ngreater than 3 across western and central MN by Tuesday night. \nAs for the severe risk, it continues to appear low but \nconditionally intriguing if the boundary layer theta-e values \ncan overachieve. Forecast soundings are moist adiabatic, \npossibly as a result of heavy rain rates in the model, but \nsurface temps would need to be about 15 degrees warmer to result\nin any upright instability. Given poor lapse rates aloft, even \nthe general thunder threat is fairly low. \n\nModel consistency drops off after Tuesday and differences \nin the evolution of the upper low become apparent. Some models\nlinger showery precip into Wednesday, some even longer. Split\nflow returns late next week with no discernible sensible weather\nfeatures to track.",
